  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather through the weekend with chilly/frosty \nnights and sunny/mild afternoons. Lows mainly in the 20s & 30s with \nhighs in the upper 50s-lower 60s Sat and well into the 60s Sun.\n\nDistant offshore ocean storm will continue to lift NE and move \nfurther way losing its grip on our region. Lingering gusty winds \nacross the Cape/Islands will diminish early this evening. \nOtherwise...a large 1035+ mb high pressure system will build \noverhead from the west through the weekend. The result will be \nmainly clear skies with chilly/frosty nights and mild sunny \nafternoons. Excellent radiational cooling conditions will allow for \nlows to drop into the 20s across the normally colder outlying \nlocations with mainly 30s elsewhere. Meanwhile the Urban Heat Island \nof Boston will probably not drop below 40. Highs Sat will recover \nnicely into the upper 50s and lower 60s and well into the 60s Sun. \nWinds will also be rather light resulting in comfortable afternoons \nfor the latter half of October.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure dominates through next week, \nbringing plenty of sunny days and cooler nights.\n\nStrong high pressure will prevail across New England for most of \nnext week. Mostly sunny skies, dry conditions, and mostly calm winds \nare expected for a majority of the week, leading to good radiational \ncooling most nights. Day time temperatures are likely to remain in \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s and nighttime temps generally in the low to \nmid 30s for most of southern New England.\n\nTwo exceptions to the pleasant conditions: a weak cold front drops \ninto the region Tuesday, leading to some gusty winds Tuesday \nafternoon/evening along the Cape/Islands/SE MA and cooler \ntemperatures behind the front on Wednesday (likely in the low 50s). \nThis front looks to be moisture-starved and not likely to produce \nany showers at this time.\n\nA second cold front looks to sweep through sometime Thursday \nnight/early Friday morning, bringing some slightly increased PoPs \n(10-20) to portions of SNE.",
